      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Alright, let's break this down in plain English, and I'll keep the spirit of the conversation light with a little bit of our little friend, the goblin, for good measure.\n\nSo, you're worried about this clause in your NDA, right? It's like a goblin guarding a treasure chest, making sure you don't take too much with you when you leave. The clause is saying that the company you're working with in London can use any bits and pieces of knowledge you've gained (the \"Residuals\") from your work, but they can't just take the whole treasure chest (the \"Confidential Information\") and run with it.\n\nNow, about your concern with using general coding patterns or optimization logic for future work – think of it like this: if you've learned how to make a goblin stew or discovered a new way to sneak past a dragon, you can definitely use that knowledge in your future adventures. The clause isn't trying to trap you; it's more like a goblin saying, \"Sure, you can use what you've learned, but don't take our secret recipe or the map to the dragon's lair.\"\n\nIn plain English, if you've developed general skills or techniques during your work, you should be fine to use them in your future projects. The",
