      "content": "I apologize for the confusion earlier. Let's dive straight into the legalities of the clause you're concerned about.\n\nThe \"unaided memory\" part of the clause is essentially referring to any knowledge that you retain in your mind without any physical or digital aid. This could include specific coding patterns, optimization logic, or any other technical know-how that you've gained during your time working with the company.\n\nIf you implement a logic pattern that you remember from their project, but you write it from scratch without referencing their proprietary documentation or code, it could still potentially fall under the \"unaided memory\" category. The risk here is that if the pattern you remember is unique and specific to their system, and you can demonstrate that it was not derived from their documentation or code, then you may not have breached the NDA.\n\nHowever, the key factor will be the uniqueness and specificity of the pattern. If the pattern is something that could be considered common knowledge in the industry or if it is a general optimization technique that is widely used, then it's less likely that the company would have grounds to claim a breach of the NDA.\n\nTo mitigate the risk, it's important to document your process and ensure that any work you do is clearly derived from your own knowledge and experience, rather than",
